1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,940
Penny buys lunch for twenty-four dollars.

2
00:00:03,259 --> 00:00:05,669
Her account is a few dollars short.

3
00:00:05,990 --> 00:00:10,730
The bank pays it anyway, and charges her thirty-four dollars for the favour.

4
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:13,400
She pays it back three days later.

5
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:18,469
If that were a loan, it would cost about seventeen thousand percent a year.

6
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:22,789
Last episode, pay later made small debts feel free.

7
00:00:23,109 --> 00:00:24,690
Today, the opposite.

8
00:00:25,010 --> 00:00:30,000
Here are three ways the system charges the most to the people who have the least.

9
00:00:30,649 --> 00:00:32,380
Engine number one:

10
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:39,679
overdraft. When an account dips below zero, many banks will cover the purchase and charge a fee.

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:49,490
The government's consumer watchdog found the typical fee was thirty-four dollars, and most were triggered by purchases of twenty-four dollars or less.

12
00:00:49,810 --> 00:00:54,829
More than half of those negative balances were paid back within three days.

13
00:00:55,479 --> 00:00:57,439
And it isn't spread evenly.

14
00:00:57,759 --> 00:01:02,520
Roughly eight percent of accounts generate most of the overdraft fees.

15
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:05,159
People living closest to zero.

16
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:16,129
Even after many banks cut these fees, Americans still paid more than five point eight billion dollars in overdraft and bounced-payment fees in twenty twenty-three.

17
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:19,250
And that's after the fees came down.

18
00:01:19,569 --> 00:01:26,010
Before the pandemic, big banks were collecting eleven to twelve billion dollars a year from these fees.

19
00:01:26,659 --> 00:01:28,409
Engine number two:

20
00:01:28,730 --> 00:01:37,519
fast cash. When rent is due before payday, and there's no savings to fall back on, a payday loan can look like a lifeline.

21
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:43,409
A typical one charges fifteen dollars for every hundred borrowed, due in two weeks.

22
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:46,780
Fifteen percent doesn't sound outrageous.

23
00:01:47,430 --> 00:01:49,180
But that's for just two weeks.

24
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:53,700
Stretched across a year, it works out to almost four hundred percent.

25
00:01:54,019 --> 00:02:00,849
And if Penny can't repay it all on payday, she can pay another fee to roll it over, and the clock starts again.

26
00:02:01,170 --> 00:02:03,769
The loan was supposed to bridge the gap.

27
00:02:04,090 --> 00:02:06,269
Instead, the gap gets wider.

28
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:08,689
Engine number three:

29
00:02:09,009 --> 00:02:10,740
not having a bank at all.

30
00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:17,349
In twenty twenty-three, about five point six million American households had no bank account.

31
00:02:17,669 --> 00:02:19,330
The most common reason?

32
00:02:19,650 --> 00:02:23,069
They didn't have enough money to meet the minimum balance.

33
00:02:23,719 --> 00:02:27,710
So the people with the least money end up paying to use their own.

34
00:02:28,030 --> 00:02:30,139
A fee to cash a paycheck.

35
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:32,080
A fee to pay a bill.

36
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:34,900
A fee to load a prepaid card.

37
00:02:35,219 --> 00:02:36,669
Each one is small.

38
00:02:36,990 --> 00:02:42,039
Together, they're a tax on being broke that people with a little cushion never pay.

39
00:02:42,689 --> 00:02:45,680
The novelist Terry Pratchett had a name for this.

40
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:47,259
The boots theory.

41
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:51,889
A rich person buys one good pair of boots that lasts for years.

42
00:02:52,210 --> 00:03:00,849
A poor person can only afford cheap boots, which fall apart, so they keep buying them, and end up spending more and still have wet feet.

43
00:03:01,169 --> 00:03:07,650
It's fiction. But overdrafts, payday loans and check-cashing fees are the real-world version.

44
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:10,009
So here's the machine.

45
00:03:10,330 --> 00:03:13,340
Overdraft charges you for being a little short.

46
00:03:13,659 --> 00:03:16,990
Fast cash charges you for being short on time.

47
00:03:17,310 --> 00:03:20,629
And no bank charges you for being short, full stop.

48
00:03:20,949 --> 00:03:24,129
The cheapest thing you can own is a small cushion.

49
00:03:24,449 --> 00:03:32,580
One: turn off overdraft coverage, so a short balance means a declined card, not a thirty-four dollar fee.

50
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:37,569
Two: look for a free checking account with no minimum balance.

51
00:03:37,889 --> 00:03:43,229
Three: start an emergency fund, even if it's just ten dollars a week.

52
00:03:43,550 --> 00:03:46,789
The first five hundred dollars does the most work.

53
00:03:47,439 --> 00:03:52,599
Being broke is expensive because the system prices in your lack of options.

54
00:03:52,919 --> 00:03:56,930
But there's one con that starts with a simple wrong-number text.

55
00:03:57,250 --> 00:04:01,050
How the pig-butchering scam works, next on Money Mechanics.

